`aes_cbc_encrypt` computes `block_count = ceil(len(data) / 16)`, so with `len(data) = 50` there are 4 iterations (`i = 0..3`). Before iteration `i == 3`, three full blocks have already been appended, and each appended `encrypted_block` comes from `aes_encrypt`, whose docstring and body operate on one 16-byte block and return a 16-item list, so `encrypted_data` is length `3 * 16 = 48` at the line just before `+=`. In iteration 3, slicing gives the last two items (`data[48:64]`), and `pad_block` pads that 2-item block to 16 items for PKCS#7-style padding; `xor` returns one item per zipped pair, so `mixed_block` is also length 16, and `aes_encrypt(mixed_block, expanded_key)` returns a 16-item `encrypted_block`.

## Answer
{"len_block":16,"len_encrypted_block":16,"len_encrypted_data":48,"len_mixed_block":16}
